Subtask 3.3: Demonstrate Practical Access Control
Start the VM as Admin User
# Start the VM (admin should be able to do this)
kubectl patch vm test-vm -n vm-lab --type merge -p '{"spec":{"running":true}}'

# Check VM status
kubectl get vm test-vm -n vm-lab
kubectl get vmi test-vm -n vm-lab
Try to Delete VM as Operator (Should Fail)
# This should fail because operators cannot delete VMs
kubectl auth can-i delete vm test-vm --as=system:serviceaccount:vm-lab:vm-operator-user -n vm-lab
View VM as Viewer User
# This should succeed - viewers can see VMs
kubectl auth can-i get vm test-vm --as=system:serviceaccount:vm-lab:vm-viewer-user -n vm-lab
Subtask 3.4: Create Advanced Role with Resource Quotas
Create a more advanced role that includes resource quota management:

Create a file named vm-resource-admin-role.yaml:

apiVersion: rbac.authorization.k8s.io/v1
kind: Role
metadata:
  namespace: vm-lab
  name: vm-resource-administrator
rules:
- apiGroups: ["kubevirt.io"]
  resources: ["virtualmachines", "virtualmachineinstances"]
  verbs: ["*"]
- apiGroups: [""]
  resources: ["pods", "services", "persistentvolumeclaims", "resourcequotas", "limitranges"]
  verbs: ["*"]
- apiGroups: ["apps"]
  resources: ["deployments", "replicasets"]
  verbs: ["get", "list", "create", "update", "patch", "delete"]
- apiGroups: ["subresources.kubevirt.io"]
  resources: ["*"]
  verbs: ["*"]
Apply the advanced role:

kubectl apply -f vm-resource-admin-role.yaml
Create a RoleBinding for this advanced role:

apiVersion: rbac.authorization.k8s.io/v1
kind: RoleBinding
metadata:
  name: vm-resource-admin-binding
  namespace: vm-lab
subjects:
- kind: ServiceAccount
  name: vm-admin-user
  namespace: vm-lab
roleRef:
  kind: Role
  name: vm-resource-administrator
  apiGroup: rbac.authorization.k8s.io
Save this as vm-resource-admin-rolebinding.yaml and apply:

kubectl apply -f vm-resource-admin-rolebinding.yaml
